<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintana Roo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintana Roo is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast. It holds Maya sites such as Tulum, Cobá and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzibanche">Dzibanche</a>, and was long a stronghold of rebel Maya during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> War. Made a federal territory in 1902, it became a state in 1974 and is now a centre of tourism around Cancún.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quintana-roo.jpg" alt="Quintana Roo" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heraldry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Quintana_Roo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Popol Vuh belongs to the Kʼicheʼ people of Guatemala, one of the Maya peoples, who also live in the Mexican states of Chiapas, Campeche, Yucatan and <b>Quintana Roo</b> and in parts of Belize, Honduras and El Salvador.</blockquote><a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The modern city of Chetumal, capital of <b>Quintana Roo</b>, was founded in 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/chetumal">Chetumal</a></li><li><blockquote>It is now a popular archaeological site in <b>Quintana Roo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/coba">Cobá</a></li><li><blockquote>It lay in the forests of east-central Yucatán, in what is now <b>Quintana Roo</b>, with Tihosuco and Ichmul among its main towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/cochuah">Cochuah</a></li><li><blockquote>The snake-head emblem dynasty of Kaan is first firmly attested at Dzibanche in southern <b>Quintana Roo</b> in the 5th and 6th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a></li><li><blockquote>Uaymil emerged after the fall of the League of Mayapan around 1441 in the south-east of the peninsula, between the provinces of Cochuah and Chetumal, in what is now southern <b>Quintana Roo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/uaymil">Uaymil</a></li></ul></section>
